class.description:
The reader pulls whole bytes and hands bits back LSB-first, mirroring the writer exactly. It is a separate type rather than a second role on one object because the two carry separate cursors, and a single instance serving both would corrupt each with the other's accumulator.
A read past the end yields zero bytes rather than failing, so a truncated packet decodes to values the caller can reject instead of stopping the decode.
[codeblock]
var r := NetwBitBufferReader.create(bytes)
var tick := r.get_aligned_u32()
var x := r.get_bits(11)
var y := r.get_bits(11)
[/codeblock]
Widths come from the shared synchronizer config on both peers, never the wire, so a one-bit offset cannot drift between encoder and decoder.
